From: "Pluet, LLC" <358065800+pluetllc@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] stream_lavf: read large http(s) files through several
 connections

A single TCP connection over a long or lossy path carries only so much:
on a remote media server one connection often gives a fraction of the
line, and a high-bitrate remux then buffers while the line sits idle.
stream/http_parallel.c reads the file through several connections
instead, with the same options (headers, cookies, TLS, timeouts,
FFmpeg's reconnect) as the stream layer's own.

The file is cut into chunks (--stream-http-chunk-size, 2 MiB). A worker
thread per connection (--stream-http-connections, 4; 1 turns it off)
fetches whole chunks with ranged requests (FFmpeg's offset and
end_offset) into a few chunk buffers the reader copies from; bytes are
handed over as they arrive, so the chunk the reader is in costs no more
latency than one connection does, and a reader that lands inside a chunk
is served from where it landed. Until it has read on, the reader's chunk is
fetched on one open-ended reply that runs on into the next chunks, as a
single connection would. Once the reader has read on 4 MiB from one
position (past a file's headers and the first frames after a seek, so a
start or a seek never shares the line with read-ahead), the chunks ahead
of it are fetched too, one per connection.
A seek moves the window: fetches the reader left are stopped (their bytes
are kept), and fetched chunks stay until their buffer is needed, so the
demuxer's reads of a header, an index at the end and the start again
cost nothing twice. The connection the stream layer opened becomes the
first worker's. Memory: connections + 3 chunk buffers (14 MiB at the
defaults).

Connections carry on from where their last reply ended: FFmpeg's http
protocol (patches 0080/0081) continues a bounded reply on the same
connection, or sends the next ranged request over it once a reply was
read to its end. A failed fetch is retried with backoff (0.25, 0.5, 1 s)
on a new connection, after FFmpeg's own reconnects; a server that refuses
another connection while others work (a per-client limit) is read through
as many as work from then on.

Only for read-only http:// and https:// (and dav/webdav) files whose size
is known, at least 32 MiB and seekable, and never for loopback hosts (an
app's own byte server for shares). Live streams, HLS (opened by
libavformat) and everything else read as before. The bytes the demuxer
gets are the same, so exact seeks are unchanged.

Measured with mpv (macOS, --vo=null --ao=null) on a 24 Mbit/s Matroska
file from a server on the LAN, 1 -> 4 connections, 2-3 runs each:
- each connection limited to 1.5 MB/s, 60 ms before each reply: start
  2.20 -> 1.44 s, cache after 10 s 0.1 -> 6 s, cache-pauses in 30 s
  7 -> 1; resume at 50 s unchanged (1.72-1.76 -> 1.75-1.76 s), with
  6 s cached after 10 s instead of none;
- one 4 MB/s line shared by every connection: start 1.12-1.16 ->
  0.77-0.79 s, resume at 50 s unchanged (0.91-0.94 -> 0.94-0.95 s), cache
  after 10 s 0.9 -> 4 s from the start;
- unthrottled: the same (start 0.04 s, cache full either way).
scripts/test_http_parallel.sh checks every byte through the stream
layer's walk, retries and a server's connection limit, and prints the
speedup (24 MiB at 2 MB/s per connection: 3.5x with 4).
